NomadFS is a personal filesystem for the devices you own: a laptop, a phone,
a home server. Each device keeps its own files. What you share is listed on
every device at once, and a file's bytes travel only when someone opens it.

It is early software. The node, the command line and the Linux mount run on
Linux; the app runs on the Linux desktop and on Android phones. See the
[roadmap](https://github.com/highercomve/nomadfs/blob/main/ROADMAP.md) for
where it is going.

# [Start]($heading.id('start'))

[Getting started]($link.page('docs/getting-started')) builds NomadFS, writes a
configuration, shares a folder and brings a second device into the swarm.

# [Using NomadFS]($heading.id('using'))

- [Shared folders & mount]($link.page('docs/shared-folders')): what a shared
  folder is, how devices browse it, what downloads when, and the Linux FUSE
  mount.
- [Swarm folder]($link.page('docs/swarm-folder')): the one folder every
  device reads and writes, how edits merge and what happens on a conflict.
- [Connecting devices]($link.page('docs/connecting-devices')): swarms, one-time
  invites and QR codes, trusted devices and revocation.

# [Words used here]($heading.id('words'))

- **Swarm**: all the devices that hold the same swarm key. They can reach each
  other and see each other's shares.
- **Share**: a folder on one device, under a name, that the rest of the swarm
  can browse.
- **Swarm folder**: one folder that belongs to the whole swarm rather than to
  one device.
- **Mount**: on Linux, all of it as an ordinary folder: `<mount>/<device>/<share>`
  and `<mount>/Swarm`.
